{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":false,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":5,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"id":"5b4d3b0a","cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Day 8/365 — Classification vs Regression\n\n## Goal\n\nThis notebook demonstrates the difference between two major supervised-learning problems:\n\n1. **Classification** — predict a category  \n   Example: Will a borrower **default or not default**?\n\n2. **Regression** — predict a continuous value  \n   Example: What is the **estimated house price**?\n\nTo keep the notebook reproducible and runnable even without internet access, both datasets below are **synthetic educational datasets**.\n\n> **Important:** The generated financial and housing data do not represent real customers, real underwriting rules, real property valuations, or production decision systems.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"6e283144","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Why this experiment matters\n\nThe important difference is not just the algorithm.\n\nIt starts with the **target variable**:\n\n- If the target is a **class/category**, we have a classification problem.\n- If the target is a **number on a continuous scale**, we have a regression problem.\n\nWe will use realistic-looking features, train a simple baseline model for each task, and visualize what each model is actually trying to predict.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"0688112f","cell_type":"code","source":"import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression, LinearRegression\nfrom sklearn.metrics import (\n    accuracy_score,\n    classification_report,\n    ConfusionMatrixDisplay,\n    RocCurveDisplay,\n    mean_absolute_error,\n    mean_squared_error,\n    r2_score\n)\n\nnp.random.seed(42)\nprint(\"Libraries loaded successfully.\")\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-08T18:05:02.030611Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-08T18:05:02.031564Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-08T18:05:02.039317Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-08-08T18:05:02.031515Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-08T18:05:02.038083Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Libraries loaded successfully.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":6},{"id":"a1afe42d","cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Part 1 — Classification\n\n## Business question\n\n> **Will this borrower default on the next payment?**\n\nThe model will use features such as:\n\n- annual income\n- credit utilization\n- outstanding debt\n- repayment-history score\n- account age\n\nThe target is:\n\n- `0` = No default\n- `1` = Default\n\nBecause the answer belongs to one of two categories, this is a **binary classification** problem.\n","metadata":{}},{"id":"047b41c8","cell_type":"code","source":"# Generate a synthetic credit-risk-style dataset\n\nn = 1500\n\nincome = np.random.normal(70000, 22000, n).clip(18000, 180000)\nutilization = np.random.beta(2.2, 2.5, n)\noutstanding_debt = np.random.normal(22000, 12000, n).clip(500, 90000)\nrepayment_score = np.random.beta(5, 2, n)\naccount_age_months = np.random.randint(3, 180, n)\n\n# Construct a latent default-risk score.\n# This is only for simulation, not a real-world credit formula.\nrisk_score = (\n    2.7 * utilization\n    + 0.000018 * outstanding_debt\n    - 2.8 * repayment_score\n    - 0.000006 * income\n    - 0.0025 * account_age_months\n    + np.random.normal(0, 0.45, n)\n)\n\ndefault = (risk_score > 0.15).astype(int)\n\ncredit_df = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"annual_income\": income.round(0),\n    \"credit_utilization\": utilization.round(3),\n    \"outstanding_debt\": outstanding_debt.round(0),\n    \"repayment_score\": repayment_score.round(3),\n    \"account_age_months\": account_age_months,\n    \"default\": default\n})\n\ncredit_df.head()\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-08T18:05:02.041153Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-08T18:05:02.041983Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-08T18:05:02.103423Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-08-08T18:05:02.04193Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-08T18:05:02.102435Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":7,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"   annual_income  credit_utilization  outstanding_debt  repayment_score  \\\n0        80928.0               0.684           13979.0            0.800   \n1        66958.0               0.512           41175.0            0.444   \n2        84249.0               0.279           14231.0            0.721   \n3       103507.0               0.716           30291.0            0.682   \n4        64849.0               0.745           22216.0            0.592   \n\n   account_age_months  default  \n0                 163        0  \n1                  98        1  \n2                 124        0  \n3                  27        0  \n4                 144        1  ","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>annual_income</th>\n      <th>credit_utilization</th>\n      <th>outstanding_debt</th>\n      <th>repayment_score</th>\n      <th>account_age_months</th>\n      <th>default</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>80928.0</td>\n      <td>0.684</td>\n      <td>13979.0</td>\n      <td>0.800</td>\n      <td>163</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>66